1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,218
This VR demo is twenty-two gigabytes.
The store page asks you to free up nine.

2
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:09,432
And eighteen and a half of those gigabytes are the same thing,
six times over.

3
00:00:13,282 --> 00:00:15,025
Let's do the game first.

4
00:00:15,275 --> 00:00:21,903
Prenecrotic: The Cursed School. You are in a headset,
in an abandoned school, looking for your wife.

5
00:00:22,153 --> 00:00:28,729
It is VR only. No flat screen mode, no keyboard.
If you don't own a headset there is nothing here for you.

6
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:32,629
And it commits to that.
Watch what your hands are made to do.

7
00:00:32,879 --> 00:00:40,840
You hold a paper map up to a flashlight to read it.
You wind a record player. You punch a code into a keypad, one finger at a time.

8
00:00:46,090 --> 00:00:52,980
That is the argument for horror in VR. The fear isn't the thing in the room.
It's that your hands are busy when it arrives.

9
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:58,683
There's a bat early on,
and later a pistol with fifteen rounds you can count in the corner of your eye.

10
00:00:58,933 --> 00:01:04,673
It went up on Steam yesterday. Ten languages,
no reviews, and a full game with no date on it.

11
00:01:04,923 --> 00:01:06,353
Which leaves the build.

12
00:01:06,603 --> 00:01:12,056
Twenty-two point two eight gigabytes.
One thousand one hundred and seventy-one files.

13
00:01:12,306 --> 00:01:19,169
The store page's own minimum spec asks for nine gigabytes of storage.
The demo by itself is more than twice that.

14
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:23,435
So I went looking for where it all went.
It isn't the art.

15
00:01:23,685 --> 00:01:29,843
Unity's Addressables system packs content into bundles,
and this build's bundles sit in one folder.

16
00:01:30,093 --> 00:01:37,270
Six of them carry the same group name.
Six different hashes. Three point zero nine gigabytes each.

17
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:45,063
That trailing hash is a content hash.
Six hashes means six builds of one group — not six sets of scenes.

18
00:01:45,313 --> 00:01:52,542
Every rebuild writes a new bundle beside the old one,
and nothing deletes the old one. Then the whole folder gets packed.

19
00:01:52,792 --> 00:01:56,260
One of the six is loaded.
The other five are last week.

20
00:01:56,510 --> 00:02:00,160
Eighteen and a half gigabytes of previous builds,
in a demo.

21
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:01,918
Then there is this one.

22
00:02:02,168 --> 00:02:07,908
Unity writes that folder name itself.
Back up this folder, but don't ship it with your game.

23
00:02:08,158 --> 00:02:18,861
One thousand and sixty-nine files. Seven hundred and fifty C++ files that IL2CPP generated out of their C#,
and the managed assemblies beside them.

24
00:02:19,111 --> 00:02:27,072
If you saw the Frontier Tale short yesterday, that is the same folder.
Different studio, different engine, same unticked box.

25
00:02:27,322 --> 00:02:32,592
There's a second one next to it,
and Unity named that one Do Not Ship. Also shipped.

26
00:02:32,842 --> 00:02:38,216
Take out the duplicates and those two folders,
and the game is about three point seven gigabytes.

27
00:02:38,466 --> 00:02:41,516
Five sixths of what you
download never runs.

28
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:44,998
The VR side has its own
version of the same story.

29
00:02:45,248 --> 00:02:54,045
SteamVR, Meta's plugin, and Unity's OpenXR are all in there.
Three routes into a headset, and a player uses one.

30
00:02:54,295 --> 00:03:00,558
There's also an ARM64 folder holding the editor's fake headset,
in a game that only ships for Windows.

31
00:03:00,808 --> 00:03:02,656
And two I can't account for.

32
00:03:02,906 --> 00:03:11,990
Thirty-four megabytes of OpenCV — computer vision — and forty-nine of DLSS.
Then Meta's XR agent bridge, which is a thing you drive the editor with.

33
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:19,757
Two episodes ago I found an agent bridge in another horror demo and said I'd rather be wrong out loud.
Same offer here.

34
00:03:20,007 --> 00:03:23,475
Reception. There isn't any yet —
it's a day old.

35
00:03:23,725 --> 00:03:34,428
So here's the honest read. The game underneath is more careful than the build around it.
The hands do real work, the school is a good place to be lost in, and somebody spent time on it.

36
00:03:34,678 --> 00:03:38,015
What shipped around it is a
first build that nobody swept.

37
00:03:38,265 --> 00:03:40,609
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

38
00:03:40,859 --> 00:03:50,230
Unity on IL2CPP, twenty-two gigabytes, eighteen of them stale,
three VR runtimes, and a folder that asked not to come.

39
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:57,657
Worth a dip if you own a headset. It's free,
it's genuinely unpleasant in the way it wants to be, and it's about half an hour.

40
00:03:57,907 --> 00:04:11,641
If you ship Unity in VR, take three things from this one. Clear the Addressables folder between builds.
Delete the two folders Unity told you to delete. Pick one XR path. That's eighteen gigabytes of somebody's download.

41
00:04:11,891 --> 00:04:22,464
That's my read from the outside. If you worked on this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
